# 1️⃣ What is a File System?

A **File System** is the OS component responsible for:

* Creating files
* Deleting files
* Organising files into directories
* Managing storage on disks
* Controlling file access and permissions

Examples:

* FAT32
* NTFS
* ext4

---

# 2️⃣ File Allocation Methods

## A. Contiguous Allocation

Each file occupies consecutive disk blocks.

### Advantages

* Fast sequential and random access.
* Simple implementation.

### Disadvantages

* External fragmentation.
* Difficult to grow files.

---

## B. Linked Allocation

Each file is stored as a linked list of disk blocks.

### Advantages

* No external fragmentation.
* Easy file expansion.

### Disadvantages

* Slow random access.
* Pointer overhead.

---

## C. Indexed Allocation

Each file has an **index block** containing addresses of all file blocks.

### Advantages

* Fast direct access.
* No external fragmentation.

### Disadvantages

* Extra memory needed for index blocks.

---

# 3️⃣ Directory Structures

### Single-Level Directory

* One directory for all users.
* Simple but causes filename conflicts.

### Two-Level Directory

* Separate directory for each user.
* Eliminates filename conflicts.

### Tree-Structured Directory

* Hierarchical structure.
* Most commonly used.

### Acyclic Graph Directory

* Allows shared files/directories.
* No cycles.

---

# 4️⃣ Disk Scheduling

When multiple disk access requests exist, the OS decides the servicing order to minimise seek time.

---

# 5️⃣ FCFS (First Come, First Served)

Requests are served in arrival order.

### Advantages

* Simple
* Fair

### Disadvantages

* Poor average seek time.

---

# 6️⃣ SSTF (Shortest Seek Time First)

Services the request closest to the current head position.

### Advantages

* Better performance than FCFS.

### Disadvantages

* Far-away requests may starve.

---

# 7️⃣ SCAN (Elevator Algorithm)

The disk head moves in one direction, servicing all requests, then reverses direction.

Advantages:

* Good throughput.
* Less starvation.

---

# 8️⃣ C-SCAN (Circular SCAN)

The head moves in one direction only.

After reaching the end, it jumps back to the beginning without servicing requests on the return.

Advantages:

* More uniform waiting time.

---

# 9️⃣ LOOK Algorithm

Similar to SCAN, but the head reverses at the **last pending request**, not at the physical end of the disk.

Advantage:

* Reduces unnecessary movement.

---

# 🔟 C-LOOK Algorithm

Similar to C-SCAN, but after servicing the last request, the head jumps directly to the first pending request.

Advantage:

* Less head movement than C-SCAN.

---

# 1️⃣1️⃣ Comparison of Disk Scheduling Algorithms

| Algorithm | Average Seek Time  | Starvation | Fairness  |
| --------- | ------------------ | ---------- | --------- |
| FCFS      | High               | No         | High      |
| SSTF      | Low                | Yes        | Medium    |
| SCAN      | Low                | Rare       | Good      |
| C-SCAN    | Low                | No         | Very Good |
| LOOK      | Better than SCAN   | Rare       | Good      |
| C-LOOK    | Better than C-SCAN | No         | Very Good |

